course: dictionary
lesson: 4551
title: Heatstroke: when the body overheats
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Heatstroke is a dangerous illness. It happens when your body gets far too hot, from hot sun or hard exercise in heat, and cannot cool itself down. «Heat exhaustion can turn into heatstroke, a medical emergency.» So the early signs matter.
explain: Use the context clues. Words like «emergency», «confused» and «fainting» show that heatstroke is serious. «Someone in the heat who is confused, very hot and has stopped sweating may have heatstroke, which is an emergency: call the emergency number.» The sentence even tells you what to do.
explain: Heat exhaustion is the milder stage: you feel weak, dizzy and sick. If it is not treated, it can become heatstroke. Compare the two words in the sentences to see which one is more serious: heatstroke is the one where the body can no longer cool itself.
fact: Heatstroke is a dangerous illness when your body gets far too hot and cannot cool itself down.
fact: Signs of heatstroke include very hot, dry or red skin, a headache, confusion and fainting.
fact: To prevent heatstroke, drink water, wear a hat, stay in the shade and rest in the hottest part of the day.
fact: If someone has heatstroke, call the emergency services, move them into the shade and cool them with water.
fact: Never leave children or pets in a hot car, because heatstroke can cause death without help.
exercise: What does heatstroke mean? | *A dangerous illness when your body gets far too hot and cannot cool itself down | A type of sun cream that stops sunburn | A hot drink that helps you sleep | A cold feeling after swimming in the sea
exercise: «Someone in the heat who is confused, very hot and has stopped sweating may have heatstroke.» What should you do first? | Tell them to run around to get more exercise | *Call the emergency number | Leave them alone to rest in the sun | Give them a big hot meal
exercise: Which is a sign of heatstroke? | Wanting to sleep with a blanket | *Very hot, dry or red skin | Cold, blue fingers | Itchy spots on the arms
test: Name one way to prevent heatstroke on a hot day. | Wear a thick winter coat | *Drink plenty of water | Stay out in the midday sun all afternoon | Run hard for an hour with no break
test: Why must children never be left in a parked car on a hot day? | Because the car will get dirty | Because the car might roll away | *Because the car heats up fast and can cause heatstroke | Because it is too dark inside
exam: What is the milder stage of overheating, which can turn into heatstroke if not treated? | Hay fever | *Heat exhaustion | Sunburn | Hypothermia
